 descendants and i  think maybe a second rule that is  helpful if you are in the in just one  hierarchy in the tree so if you have  siblings if you want  avoid spill over unless you know that  these citrix do exclude like in case of  of ce 1956 for instance i think these  two rules  nearly cover it all already  thank you michael the next question here  is from matthew again  for a cytoflex cytometer can i take a  filter from the red laser chamber and  place it into the yellow laser chamber  thank you  um absolutely the  the filters that are used in the  cytoflex can be moved interchangeably  between different spots so that you can  create custom  configurations that includes moving them  between pods the the mounting apparatus  for each of the filters is the same  across the board so there's no issue  with moving them however you see fit  all right thank you matthew i think the  next question here is from michael  regarding stacking of antibodies is  there a limit of how many antibodies can  be stacked using the same fluorochrome  well  i would say i don't know actually but i  did not run into this yet just to give  you an example um  we just have developed a kit for inner  limb for itself staining where we have  stacked 11 antibodies in one channel and  we do not face any issues i think  specifically when you when you want to  create the so-called dump channels so  when you want to exclude certain  lineages  from your your further analysis  i don't see that you quickly would run  into into  an issue here um i think  the the  limiting factor maybe is more what makes  sense  so this is probably more limiting than  the technical aspect here  thank you michael our next question is  there a significant difference in the  spillover seen in tandem dies from lot  to lot asr antibodies for the same  target antigen and obtained from the  same manufacturer  yeah it depends a little bit on what you  consider a significant difference  typically you would see a lot a lot  differences  which is basically  related to the fact that  it's impossible  to  generate two lots of tendon dyes that  are exactly the same you  can tailor the manufacturing process in  a way that these two lots come very  close to each other and that is  typically seen if you have  manufacturers that follow iso 9001  processes cgmp-like processes that  really try to avoid any variability and  and problems  but  i would say a hundred percent  uh identical tandem dies from two  different lots are more like the  exception  thank you michael looks like we have one  more question here  is there a typical bc set of  fluorochromes conjugated to antibodies  recognizing the main types of leukocytes  yes and no  so again this goes a little bit along  what is apparent what is a strong  marker what is a rather meat marker so  here if you say  main type of leukocytes we probably look  into villages  so chrome orange for instance is a  relatively dim dye we use this for a pan  glucoside parent marker cd45  for the dura clone kits we have used a  con a uniform setting where all the  t-cell gating markers 3 4 and 8 are  sitting on the red laser with a lot of  spill over but as these markers are  either extruding or apparent  are really really strongly expressed uh  this gives us a good a good separation  still while these red dyes do not  influence the other lasers a lot so  these are the considerations that you  might take into account here a real  typical set  maybe a cd45 from orange that is maybe a  real typical choice  all right thank you michael thank you  both again um michael and matthew do  either of you have final comments for  our audience  yeah uh thanks for asking so there were  a number of questions that were asked  that  we're going to follow up with those  individual questions by email the the  answer to them would have been very  complex and  a little bit of opinion so  we will respond to your questions in a  timely fashion and we thank you for  offering them even if we